{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on 718769a3: the authoring boundary is sufficient for head\nF. The rule reaches the fold at the identifiers cutover, not in F.\n\nYour finding is right. The fold cannot tell a new declaration from\na recorded one, so a refusal at the raw call sites would break the\nhistory that 0a0286c4 protects.\n\n1. The rule lives in the kernel's validator as the explicit mode\n   that you propose: one pure canonical-spelling helper, used by\n   `validateDefinition(..., { canonical: true })` and by Define's\n   `normalize`. The default stays off. `definitionDigest` does not\n   change.\n2. In F the mode is on in two places:\n   - Define's `compile`, which normalizes once and validates the\n     canonical output.\n   - The test of request item 3, over every pinned and generated\n     definition.\n3. Also turn the mode on where the CLI reads an author's source\n   files to check or pack an application\n   (`packages/cli/src/application-files.ts`), but only if that call\n   never reads a recorded or signed package. If it does, leave the\n   CLI as it is and say so in the note. Do not ask again for that\n   choice.\n4. No fold call site changes in F: scope creation, application\n   install, the rules scope, the directory, archive reads and\n   Replay keep the default. A room can still be created from the\n   recorded demo release.\n5. The review under docs/kernel.md section 7 records the decision\n   of request item 1 as \"refuse, in canonical mode\" and gives the\n   reason for the staging: one meaning has one identity for every\n   definition that the tools produce from F, and for every\n   definition that a room admits from the cutover.\n\nThe cutover is where the fold starts to refuse. New-format rooms\nhave no recorded history in the old spelling, so each fold call\nsite can pass `canonical: true` without a second code line. I add\nthat item to the identifiers build under 05d618e2. Include it in\nyour head plan for that build.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}